ts{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"parents":[{"sha":"eb05edb2b36e5b09529bbfb1811c9b31786d25fb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/commits/eb05edb2b36e5b09529bbfb1811c9b31786d25fb","html_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/commit/eb05edb2b36e5b09529bbfb1811c9b31786d25fb"},{"sha":"88e6f4de88d8622350355cb27083fb433870e759","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/commits/88e6f4de88d8622350355cb27083fb433870e759","html_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/commit/88e6f4de88d8622350355cb27083fb433870e759"}]}],"files":[{"sha":"f713fe776242bc0d0e8678fd6674a30a1669feaf","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S1/Dynamics/FiniteFiberEntropy.md","status":"added","additions":29,"deletions":0,"changes":29,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS1%2FDynamics%2FFiniteFiberEntropy.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS1%2FDynamics%2FFiniteFiberEntropy.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS1%2FDynamics%2FFiniteFiberEntropy.md?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@\n+# Finite Fibers Preserve Topological Entropy\n+\n+## Abstract\n+\n+A compact factor map with a uniform bound on its finite fibers preserves topological entropy.\n+\n+Let X and Y be compact uniform spaces, with Y Hausdorff. Let S:X→X be continuous, and let π:X→Y be a continuous surjection satisfying π∘S=T∘π. Every fiber π⁻¹{y} is finite and its cardinality is at most the same natural number M. The covering entropy of S equals that of T. This formalizes the classical finite-to-one factor theorem; no literature novelty is claimed.\n+\n+**Theorem 1.1 (Entropy equality under uniformly finite fibers).**\n+\n+$$\\begin{aligned}\\forall X: Type, \\forall Y: Type,\\\\{[\\operatorname{UniformSpace}(X)] [\\operatorname{CompactSpace}(X)] [\\operatorname{UniformSpace}(Y)] [\\operatorname{CompactSpace}(Y)] [\\operatorname{T2Space}(Y)],}\\\\\\forall S: X \\to X, \\forall T: Y \\to Y, \\forall \\pi: X \\to Y,\\\\\\operatorname{Continuous}(S) \\Rightarrow \\operatorname{Continuous}(\\pi) \\Rightarrow\\\\\\operatorname{Function.Surjective}(\\pi) \\Rightarrow \\operatorname{Function.Semiconj}(\\pi, S, T) \\Rightarrow\\\\\\forall M: \\mathbb{N},\\\\{\\forall y: Y, \\operatorname{Set.Finite}(\\operatorname{Set.preimage}(\\pi, \\operatorname{Set.singleton}(y))) \\land \\operatorname{Set.ncard}(\\operatorname{Set.preimage}(\\pi, \\operatorname{Set.singleton}(y))) \\le M} \\Rightarrow\\\\\\operatorname{Dynamics.coverEntropy}(S, \\operatorname{Set.univ}()) = \\operatorname{Dynamics.coverEntropy}(T, \\operatorname{Set.univ}())\\end{aligned}$$\n+\n+*Proof.* Machine-checked in Lean as `D5/S1/Dynamics/FiniteFiberEntropy.finite_fiber_entropy` (`✓ std3`). ∎\n+\n+*Source.* Repository-derived.\n+\n+*Acknowledgement.* Rufus Bowen (1971). *Entropy for group endomorphisms and homogeneous spaces*. DOI: [10.1090/S0002-9947-1971-0274707-X](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1971-0274707-X). URL: <https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1971-0274707-X>.\n+\n+*Commentary.*\n+\n+For a symmetric source entourage U and fixed block length n, the n-step balls centered on a fiber cover that fiber using at most M centers. Since π is a closed map, this cover extends to the inverse image of a neighborhood of the base point. The Lebesgue number lemma makes the base entourage uniform over Y.\n+\n+Over r consecutive blocks, each base covering orbit has at most M^r center codes. One actual orbit representative from every nonempty code class gives a source cover at entourage U∘U. No continuous selection of fiber points or lifting of a prescribed orbit is assumed. The source covering number at time n*r is at most M^r times the base covering number. Monotonicity permits restriction to these multiples; their extra entropy is log(M)/n, which tends to zero.\n+\n+The reverse inequality reuses Mathlib's coverEntropy_image_le_of_uniformContinuous. The proof handles empty spaces, M=0 when both spaces are empty, and infinite entropy. It does not require T to be continuous or X to be Hausdorff. Finiteness of fibers is an explicit hypothesis: Set.ncard alone cannot express a finite bound because it is zero on an infinite set. The result supplies the entropy comparison needed by finite symbolic codings of compact dynamical systems; constructing a coding and proving its uniform fiber bound remain application-specific obligations.\n+\n+## References\n+\n+- Truth anchor: `D5/S1/Dynamics/FiniteFiberEntropy.finite_fiber_entropy`"},{"sha":"551b2cd5e6696948d51f0d0fa1ba63953d1d980d","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S1/Dynamics/FiniteFiberEntropy.scribe.cs","status":"added","additions":75,"deletions":0,"changes":75,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS1%2FDynamics%2FFiniteFiberEntropy.scribe.cs","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS1%2FDynamics%2FFiniteFiberEntropy.scribe.cs","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS1%2FDynamics%2FFiniteFiberEntropy.scribe.cs?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,75 @@\n+using static StrataLint.Scribe.DefinitionDsl;\n+using static StrataLint.Scribe.FormulaDsl;\n+using F = StrataLint.Scribe.FormulaDsl;\n+\n+namespace StrataLint.Scribe.Blueprint.D5.S1.Dynamics;\n+\n+internal sealed class FiniteFiberEntropyDocument : IScribeDocumentDefinition\n+{\n+    private static Formula Id(string s) => s == \"π\" ? Pi : F.Id(s);\n+    private static Formula Call(string s, params Formula[] args)\n+    {\n+        var name = new List<Formula>();\n+        foreach (var part in s.Split('.'))\n+        {\n+            if (name.Count > 0) name.Add(Dot);\n+            name.Add(Id(part));\n+        }\n+        var items = new List<Formula> { Seq(Operatorname, Grp(Seq([.. name]))), Open };\n+        for (var i = 0; i < args.Length; i++)\n+        {\n+            if (i > 0) items.AddRange([Comma, Sp]);\n+            items.Add(args[i]);\n+        }\n+        items.Add(Close);\n+        return Seq([.. items]);\n+    }\n+    private static Formula All(string s, Formula t) => Seq(Forall, Sp, Id(s), Colon, Sp, t, Comma);\n+    private static Formula Instance(string s, string x) => Seq(OpenBracket, Call(s, Id(x)), CloseBracket);\n+    private static Formula Fiber => Call(\"Set.preimage\", Id(\"π\"), Call(\"Set.singleton\", Id(\"y\")));\n+    private static Formula Entropy(string t) => Call(\"Dynamics.coverEntropy\", Id(t), Call(\"Set.univ\"));\n+\n+    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(\n+        \"A compact factor map with a uniform bound on its finite fibers preserves topological entropy.\",\n+        H(\"Finite Fibers Preserve Topological Entropy\"), Blocks(\n+        Paragraph(Text(\"Let X and Y be compact uniform spaces, with Y Hausdorff. Let S:X→X be continuous, \"\n+            + \"and let π:X→Y be a continuous surjection satisfying π∘S=T∘π. Every fiber π⁻¹{y} is finite \"\n+            + \"and its cardinality is at most the same natural number M. The covering entropy of S equals that of T. \"\n+            + \"This formalizes the classical finite-to-one factor theorem; no literature novelty is claimed.\")),\n+        Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create(\"finite-fiber-entropy\"),\n+            DeclarationHandle.Create(\"D5/S1/Dynamics/FiniteFiberEntropy.finite_fiber_entropy\"),\n+            H(\"Entropy equality under uniformly finite fibers\"),\n+            StatementSource.FromAuthor(Disp(new Formula.Aligned([\n+                Seq(All(\"X\", Id(\"Type\")), Sp, All(\"Y\", Id(\"Type\"))),\n+                Seq(Grp(Instance(\"UniformSpace\", \"X\"), Sp, Instance(\"CompactSpace\", \"X\"), Sp,\n+                    Instance(\"UniformSpace\", \"Y\"), Sp, Instance(\"CompactSpace\", \"Y\"), Sp, Instance(\"T2Space\", \"Y\"), Comma)),\n+                Seq(All(\"S\", Seq(Id(\"X\"), Sp, To, Sp, Id(\"X\"))), Sp,\n+                    All(\"T\", Seq(Id(\"Y\"), Sp, To, Sp, Id(\"Y\"))), Sp,\n+                    All(\"π\", Seq(Id(\"X\"), Sp, To, Sp, Id(\"Y\")))),\n+                Seq(Call(\"Continuous\", Id(\"S\")), Sp, Rightarrow, Sp,\n+                    Call(\"Continuous\", Id(\"π\")), Sp, Rightarrow),\n+                Seq(Call(\"Function.Surjective\", Id(\"π\")), Sp, Rightarrow, Sp,\n+                    Call(\"Function.Semiconj\", Id(\"π\"), Id(\"S\"), Id(\"T\")), Sp, Rightarrow),\n+                All(\"M\", Seq(Mathbb, Grp(Id(\"N\")))),\n+                Seq(Grp(All(\"y\", Id(\"Y\")), Sp, Call(\"Set.Finite\", Fiber), Sp, Land, Sp,\n+                    Call(\"Set.ncard\", Fiber), Sp, Le, Sp, Id(\"M\")), Sp, Rightarrow),\n+                Seq(Entropy(\"S\"), Sp, Eq, Sp, Entropy(\"T\"))\n+            ]))), AssessedProvenance.FromRepo(\n+                LibraryNoteRef.Create(\"D5/L/Entropy/bowen1971entropy\")), Blocks(\n+                Paragraph(Text(\"For a symmetric source entourage U and fixed block length n, the n-step balls \"\n+                    + \"centered on a fiber cover that fiber using at most M centers. Since π is a closed map, \"\n+                    + \"this cover extends to the inverse image of a neighborhood of the base point. \"\n+                    + \"The Lebesgue number lemma makes the base entourage uniform over Y.\")),\n+                Paragraph(Text(\"Over r consecutive blocks, each base covering orbit has at most M^r center codes. \"\n+                    + \"One actual orbit representative from every nonempty code class gives a source cover at entourage U∘U. \"\n+                    + \"No continuous selection of fiber points or lifting of a prescribed orbit is assumed. \"\n+                    + \"The source covering number at time n*r is at most M^r times the base covering number. \"\n+                    + \"Monotonicity permits restriction to these multiples; their extra entropy is log(M)/n, which tends to zero.\")),\n+                Paragraph(Text(\"The reverse inequality reuses Mathlib's coverEntropy_image_le_of_uniformContinuous. \"\n+                    + \"The proof handles empty spaces, M=0 when both spaces are empty, and infinite entropy. \"\n+                    + \"It does not require T to be continuous or X to be Hausdorff. Finiteness of fibers is an explicit hypothesis: \"\n+                    + \"Set.ncard alone cannot express a finite bound because it is zero on an infinite set. \"\n+                    + \"The result supplies the entropy comparison needed by finite symbolic codings of compact dynamical systems; \"\n+                    + \"constructing a coding and proving its uniform fiber bound remain application-specific obligations.\"))),\n+            DescribeRole.Theorem))));\n+}"},{"sha":"ac8668c1835defb9de87e52e66693af8b9951a23","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S3/Arith/FactorialRatio/DyadicBinomialCancellation.md","status":"added","additions":22,"deletions":0,"changes":22,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FArith%2FFactorialRatio%2FDyadicBinomialCancellation.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FArith%2FFactorialRatio%2FDyadicBinomialCancellation.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FArith%2FFactorialRatio%2FDyadicBinomialCancellation.md?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@\n+# The all-index denominator formula for OEIS A362534\n+\n+## Abstract\n+\n+For every positive index, the reduced denominator of the polarization-transfer ratio is the odd binomial gap divided by its greatest common divisor with the ceiling half-index.\n+\n+**Theorem 1.1 (Exact reduced fractions for every positive index).**\n+\n+$$\\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N},\\; \\forall m \\in \\mathbb{N},\\; \\forall B \\in \\mathbb{N},\\; \\forall v \\in \\mathbb{N},\\; \\forall b \\in \\mathbb{N},\\; \\forall G \\in \\mathbb{N},\\; (1 \\le n \\land \\left(m = n - \\operatorname{NatDiv}\\left(n, 2\\right) \\land \\left(B = \\operatorname{choose}\\left(n, \\operatorname{NatDiv}\\left(n, 2\\right)\\right) \\land \\left(v = \\operatorname{factorization}\\left(B, 2\\right) \\land \\left(b = \\operatorname{NatDiv}\\left(B, 2^{v}\\right) \\land G = \\operatorname{NatDiv}\\left(2^{n} - B, 2^{v}\\right)\\right)\\right)\\right)\\right)) \\Rightarrow (\\operatorname{num}\\left(\\operatorname{g}\\left(n\\right)\\right) = G \\land \\left(\\operatorname{den}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{f}\\left(n\\right)}{\\operatorname{g}\\left(n\\right)}\\right) = \\operatorname{NatDiv}\\left(G, \\operatorname{gcd}\\left(m, G\\right)\\right) \\land \\operatorname{num}\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{f}\\left(n\\right)}{\\operatorname{g}\\left(n\\right)}\\right) = \\operatorname{NatDiv}\\left(m \\cdot b, \\operatorname{gcd}\\left(m, G\\right)\\right)\\right))$$\n+\n+*Proof.* Machine-checked in Lean as `D5/S3/Arith/FactorialRatio/DyadicBinomialCancellation.denominator_formula` (`✓ std3`). ∎\n+\n+*Source.* Repository-derived.\n+\n+*Commentary.*\n+\n+The functions f and g are the original rational-valued OEIS formulas from SabbaPolarizationTransferRefutation. For n at least one, let k be floor(n/2), m = n-k, B = choose(n,k), v the exponent of two in B, b = B/2^v and G = (2^n-B)/2^v. Then num(g) = G, den(f/g) = G/gcd(m,G), and num(f/g) = mb/gcd(m,G). The proof derives B strictly between zero and 2^n, hence v < n, positive odd b and G, and coprimality of b and G. The binomial identity n choose(n-1,k) = mB connects the normalized ratio to the original parity definitions. These are exact unbounded arithmetic statements, including n = 1 and n = 2. They do not formalize the physical derivation of either bound. This is the formalization of an existing written correction, not an additional claim of mathematical novelty or a new finite computation.\n+\n+## References\n+\n+- Truth anchor: `D5/S3/Arith/FactorialRatio/DyadicBinomialCancellation.denominator_formula`\n+- Dependency: [D5/S0/Certificates/SabbaPolarizationTransferRefutation](../../../S0/Certificates/SabbaPolarizationTransferRefutation.md)"},{"sha":"5fa594737ec61b1ab20831cc73b4484bacc11cf0","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S3/Arith/FactorialRatio/DyadicBinomialCancellation.scribe.cs","status":"added","additions":79,"deletions":0,"changes":79,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FArith%2FFactorialRatio%2FDyadicBinomialCancellation.scribe.cs","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FArith%2FFactorialRatio%2FDyadicBinomialCancellation.scribe.cs","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FArith%2FFactorialRatio%2FDyadicBinomialCancellation.scribe.cs?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,79 @@\n+using static StrataLint.Scribe.DefinitionDsl;\n+using static StrataLint.Scribe.FormulaDsl;\n+using F = StrataLint.Scribe.FormulaDsl;\n+\n+namespa