root and following negative label, has no repeated physical label and has length at most k. The following physical labels have values -B=2p-N and b=4p-N. The root satisfies Q<=p and 3p<=N; both seeds a and b are positive and congruent to 1 modulo 4.\")),\n+                    Paragraph(Text(\"Writing s for the SMALL mass and d=B-p, the chain has s=p-a, d>=0 and s-d=b-a. The construction selects positions along the full physical k-cycle, without a unit assumption, injectivity of values, a minimal numerical period, a phase restriction or a bound on the number of SMALL labels. Strict positivity of all SMALL labels itself excludes zero values. A positive maximum of the finite source proves 3w(i)<=N for every positive value: a maximum must be LARGE, and its second successor has value 4w(i)-N and cannot exceed that maximum. A positive SMALL step doubles; a well-founded deficit Q-w(i) therefore reaches a LARGE root.\")),\n+                    Paragraph(Text(\"The chain identity is the source-derived transport equation used by deferred-prefix queues. This statement extracts each chain; it does not yet assert that the extracted chains partition all physical labels, nor construct a complete queue word. A complete permutation with bound C+floor((A-mu)/2), where C=(N-1)/2 and mu,A are native seed extrema, requires those further global steps. The exact-C positive-SMALL conclusion and full Circle Conjecture 3.1 remain open.\"))),\n+                DescribeRole.Theorem)), []));\n+}"},{"sha":"bd76c90b1179f63c067feec0efe5a7d04715fd99","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SeedQueueInvariant.md","status":"added","additions":26,"deletions":0,"changes":26,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FCombinatorics%2FCircle%2FSeedQueueInvariant.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FCombinatorics%2FCircle%2FSeedQueueInvariant.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FCombinatorics%2FCircle%2FSeedQueueInvariant.md?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@\n+# Deferred-prefix ancestor invariant\n+\n+## Abstract\n+\n+Appending a dyadic list and retaining its affordable prefix preserves ancestor heights and bounds the peak at a first omission.\n+\n+**Theorem 1.1 (Physical splitting, exact landing and the ancestor range).**\n+\n+Lean statement: `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SeedQueueInvariant.deferred_prefix_invariant`\n+\n+*Proof.* Machine-checked in Lean as `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SeedQueueInvariant.deferred_prefix_invariant` (`✓ std3`). ∎\n+\n+*Source.* Repository-derived.\n+\n+*Commentary.*\n+\n+Weights f are integers on arbitrary physical labels. A queue has ancestor range [0,D] relative to mu if, for each occurrence q, its value minus mu minus the mass preceding that occurrence lies in [0,D]. Equal values do not identify occurrences. Assume the old queue has this ancestor range and mu<=b. Append a dyadic list with seed a. If that list is nonempty, assume its birth height a-mu-mass(old) lies in [0,D]. Each appended dyadic occurrence has exactly that birth height; retaining an initial segment preserves the preceding mass of each retained occurrence.\n+\n+The recursive splitBudget consumes labels from the front while the next weight fits the remaining budget. It returns a literal prefix q and literal suffix e of old++small, with mass(q)<=b-mu. For positive weights this is the longest affordable initial segment. Set oldHeight=a-mu-mass(old). With native transport mass(small)-(B-p)=b-a and 2(C-B)=b-1, the postnegative height is h=b-mu-mass(q)>=0, and direct mass conservation gives oldHeight+mass(e)+p-B=h. If x is the first emitted label, maximality gives b-mu<mass(q)+f(x). Its remembered ancestor height delta=f(x)-mu-mass(q) lies in [0,D], implying h<f(x) and B+h<=C+floor(D/2). If e is empty, the peak is exactly oldHeight+p. If b=mu and every input weight is positive, the retained queue is empty and the landing height is zero, so every currently available occurrence is emitted.\n+\n+The arithmetic and ancestor step itself does not require positive weights; positivity is needed to interpret the split as the longest affordable prefix, to force an empty queue at final zero budget, and to control intermediate positive heights. This local theorem has no supplied completion word or recovery witness. It does not by itself derive native seed extrema, partition the native source, iterate the queue through all chains, or recover the complete inventory. NativeSeedChains supplies individual native transport chains. A full source-coupled adaptive-width theorem and the original exact-C objective remain open.\n+\n+## References\n+\n+- Truth anchor: `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SeedQueueInvariant.deferred_prefix_invariant`\n+- Dependency: [D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SourceWidth](SourceWidth.md)"},{"sha":"1a6453fa21488b47c87059bd9f7d7c7fa9a39b12","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SeedQueueInvariant.scribe.cs","status":"added","additions":20,"deletions":0,"changes":20,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FCombinatorics%2FCircle%2FSeedQueueInvariant.scribe.cs","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FCombinatorics%2FCircle%2FSeedQueueInvariant.scribe.cs","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FCombinatorics%2FCircle%2FSeedQueueInvariant.scribe.cs?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@\n+using static StrataLint.Scribe.DefinitionDsl;\n+\n+namespace StrataLint.Scribe.Blueprint.D5.S3.Combinatorics.Circle;\n+\n+internal sealed class SeedQueueInvariantDocument : IScribeDocumentDefinition\n+{\n+    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(\n+        \"Appending a dyadic list and retaining its affordable prefix preserves ancestor heights and bounds the peak at a first omission.\",\n+        H(\"Deferred-prefix ancestor invariant\"),\n+        Blocks(\n+            Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create(\"deferred-prefix-ancestor-invariant\"),\n+                DeclarationHandle.Create(\"D5/S3/Combinatorics/Circle/SeedQueueInvariant.deferred_prefix_invariant\"),\n+                H(\"Physical splitting, exact landing and the ancestor range\"),\n+                StatementSource.WithoutFormula(), AssessedProvenance.FromRepo(),\n+                Blocks(\n+                    Paragraph(Text(\"Weights f are integers on arbitrary physical labels. A queue has ancestor range [0,D] relative to mu if, for each occurrence q, its value minus mu minus the mass preceding that occurrence lies in [0,D]. Equal values do not identify occurrences. Assume the old queue has this ancestor range and mu<=b. Append a dyadic list with seed a. If that list is nonempty, assume its birth height a-mu-mass(old) lies in [0,D]. Each appended dyadic occurrence has exactly that birth height; retaining an initial segment preserves the preceding mass of each retained occurrence.\")),\n+                    Paragraph(Text(\"The recursive splitBudget consumes labels from the front while the next weight fits the remaining budget. It returns a literal prefix q and literal suffix e of old++small, with mass(q)<=b-mu. For positive weights this is the longest affordable initial segment. Set oldHeight=a-mu-mass(old). With native transport mass(small)-(B-p)=b-a and 2(C-B)=b-1, the postnegative height is h=b-mu-mass(q)>=0, and direct mass conservation gives oldHeight+mass(e)+p-B=h. If x is the first emitted label, maximality gives b-mu<mass(q)+f(x). Its remembered ancestor height delta=f(x)-mu-mass(q) lies in [0,D], implying h<f(x) and B+h<=C+floor(D/2). If e is empty, the peak is exactly oldHeight+p. If b=mu and every input weight is positive, the retained queue is empty and the landing height is zero, so every currently available occurrence is emitted.\")),\n+                    Paragraph(Text(\"The arithmetic and ancestor step itself does not require positive weights; positivity is needed to interpret the split as the longest affordable prefix, to force an empty queue at final zero budget, and to control intermediate positive heights. This local theorem has no supplied completion word or recovery witness. It does not by itself derive native seed extrema, partition the native source, iterate the queue through all chains, or recover the complete inventory. NativeSeedChains supplies individual native transport chains. A full source-coupled adaptive-width theorem and the original exact-C objective remain open.\"))),\n+                DescribeRole.Theorem)), []));\n+}"},{"sha":"2bb0792405bd116cf24dcbedd755283d01603eed","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.md","status":"added","additions":43,"deletions":0,"changes":43,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FVertexAlgebra%2FSourceBPZ%2FNormalScalarTranspose.md","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FVertexAlgebra%2FSourceBPZ%2FNormalScalarTranspose.md","contents_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/the-omega-institute/trureturing/contents/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FVertexAlgebra%2FSourceBPZ%2FNormalScalarTranspose.md?ref=b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7","patch":"@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@\n+# Original integer scalar insertion transpose\n+\n+## Abstract\n+\n+NormalScalarTranspose\n+\n+The carrier is the original finite-charge direct sum of complex oscillator polynomials. B is complex bilinear, with its input order retained. No half-current or tensor carrier, symmetry, nondegeneracy, positivity, rank restriction, or all-source invariance is assumed. The normal product and divided derivative retain the Carnahan/native adaptation attribution of FieldNormalProduct. This is a classical finite normal-order calculation; no literature novelty is claimed.\n+\n+**Definition 1.1 (insertion).**\n+\n+Lean statement: `D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.insertion`\n+\n+*Formalization.* `D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.insertion` (`✓ std3`).\n+\n+*Source.* Repository-derived.\n+\n+*Commentary.*\n+\n+For every lattice D, coordinate i, natural derivative order p and actual vertex operator F, insertion is the actual normalMinusOne of dividedDerivative p (neutralField D i) with F. Thus its source oscillator is h_i(-(p+1)); it is not a newly reflected field.\n+\n+**Theorem 1.2 (consumed_normal_product_form).**\n+\n+Lean statement: `D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.consumed_normal_product_form`\n+\n+*Proof.* Machine-checked in Lean as `D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.consumed_normal_product_form` (`✓ std3`). ∎\n+\n+*Source.* Repository-derived.\n+\n+*Commentary.*\n+\n+Fix arbitrary D, a complex bilinear B on Carrier D, a coordinate i, derivative order p, actual tail field F, an arbitrary label type with finite subset S, actual fields G(a), complex coefficients lambda(a), integer reflection constants K(a), and complex charge kappa. Assume B(h_i(k)x,y)=-B(x,h_i(-k)y) for every integer k and both inputs; B(F[t]x,y)=sum over a in S of lambda(a) B(x,G(a)[K(a)-t]y) for all t,x,y; and h_i(0)G(a)[t]x=G(a)[t]h_i(0)x+kappa G(a)[t]x for a in S. The tail premise concerns F only and assumes no normal-product transpose.\n+\n+For every integer m and inputs u,v, B(insertion(p,F)[m]u,v) equals (-1)^(p+1) times the sum over a in S of lambda(a) multiplied by: the sum over s=0,...,p of choose(p+1,s+1) B(u,insertion(s,G(a))[K(a)+p+s+2-m]v), plus kappa B(u,G(a)[K(a)+p+1-m]v). Equivalently n=p+1 and r=s+1 range through n>=1 and 1<=r<=n. The final term deletes the oscillator and never invokes a derivative of order -1.\n+\n+The actual constructor's two vector sums have finite support before scalar evaluation. With A=vectorFrequencyBound(u)+1 and C=vectorFrequencyBound(v)+1 the scalar intervals are k=1-C,...,-1 and k=0,...,A-1. The omitted derivative band -p<=k<0 has zero integer choose(-k-1,p). Finite reflection j=-k and integer Vandermonde give -choose(j-1,p)=(-1)^(p+1) sum over s=0,...,p of choose(p+1,s+1)choose(-j-1,s). Only the j=0 ordering boundary uses the charge covariance and produces the displayed kappa term.\n+\n+The consumer is the cons step of the original wordField BPZ induction: F is the tail wordField, G(a) are the finite lowered or deleted tail words, K(a) their exact opposite-mode constants, and kappa is bilinear(unitCharge i,alpha). At p=0 a singleton primary tail gives the nonprimary correction B(u,G[K+1-m]v) with coefficient -lambda*kappa. This unit does not construct the original pairing or charged-primary law, prove the word induction or permutation bridge, assemble the finite L1 source flow, or establish full VOA, CFT, fusion, string theory or AdS/CFT.\n+\n+## References\n+\n+- Truth anchor: `D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.consumed_normal_product_form`\n+- Truth anchor: `D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.insertion`\n+- Dependency: [D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/LatticeSugawaraCurrents](../LatticeSugawaraCurrents.md)"},{"sha":"1060107778ba5fb73ea48a62d43aec173ba3a199","filename":"Blueprint/D5/S3/VertexAlgebra/SourceBPZ/NormalScalarTranspose.scribe.cs","status":"added","additions":26,"deletions":0,"changes":26,"blob_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/blob/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e3b6834685c7/Blueprint%2FD5%2FS3%2FVertexAlgebra%2FSourceBPZ%2FNormalScalarTranspose.scribe.cs","raw_url":"https://github.com/the-omega-institute/trureturing/raw/b62773edfb6a6f7806de09b593b2e